# 2026.10.17 · THE CLASS, not another instance. Three times a page the operator held was older than the
# page the site served — the living document (r143), the operator deck (r.202), and R-CORE on his phone the
# morning after DD-81 shipped. Each earlier time the answer was one more no-cache line for one more file, so
# a surface earned the rule by being complained about.
#
# THE RULE IS NOT SET HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. It was, for about an hour, and the live probe in
# verify-live.yml caught what that did: Workers Static Assets CONCATENATES the Cache-Control of every
# matching rule, so `/*` + `/_next/static/*` produced
#     no-cache, must-revalidate, public, max-age=31536000, immutable
# on every hashed bundle — and no-cache wins, so the immutable year was defeated. The document rule now
# lives in worker.js, where `headers.set` REPLACES and keys on the response being text/html. This block
# carries security headers only. Held by frontend/tests/cache-headers.test.mjs. Three times a page the operator held was older than the
# page the site served — the living document (r143), the operator deck (r.202), and R-CORE on his phone the
# morning after DD-81 shipped, where his own screenshot showed the </> chip still inside the mode strip:
# the layout that revision had removed. Each earlier time the answer was one more no-cache line for one more
# file, so a surface earned the rule by being complained about. It is the catch-all's job.
# `no-cache` does not mean "do not store" — it means revalidate before reuse, so a reload always gets the
# build that is actually deployed. Everything that deliberately wants a long cache is declared BELOW this
# block (/_next/static/*, /audio/*, the pdf worker at the foot), so a more specific rule wins whichever way
# overlapping rules resolve. Held by frontend/tests/cache-headers.test.mjs.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(self), geolocation=(self)

# r.202 · THE OPERATOR DECK — never cached, always the current revision (operator 2026-10-12: his phone's tab held r0.198
# while the site served r0.201 — "why are you unable to delete old file and use this?"). Same rule as the living document:
# no-cache means revalidate before reuse, so a reload always gets the revision that is actually deployed. The QR link
# (play.html?range=…) is the same path and takes the same rule.
/drone-2525/play.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

# r143 · THE LIVING DOCUMENT — never cached, always the current release.
# The operator's rule is that the HTML they hold and the HTML the site serves are
# the same exact content. A CDN or browser cache holding yesterday's build breaks
# that rule without anyone editing a byte, which is exactly how a push once read as
# shipped while the site served a four-hour-old copy. no-cache does not mean "do not
# store" — it means "revalidate before reuse", so a reader always gets the release
# that is actually deployed.
/whitepaper/vision-2525.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8

# The download copy: same bytes, forced to SAVE rather than render. Content-Disposition
# is the only mechanism every browser honours — the HTML download attribute is ignored
# cross-origin and unreliable on iOS Safari, which is where the operator reads.
/whitepaper/SOI_VISION2525_v.19_LIVING_DOCUMENT.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="SOI_VISION2525_v.19_LIVING_DOCUMENT.html"

/_next/static/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/audio/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
  Content-Type: audio/mpeg
  Accept-Ranges: bytes

# exec r1.007 · the Executive Summary's download twins — byte-identical copies of
# each language's page, forced to SAVE. Same rationale as the living document's
# download copy above: Content-Disposition is the only mechanism every browser
# honours; the download attribute is unreliable on iOS Safari.
/whitepaper/download/*
  Content-Disposition: attachment
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate

# Sensor Fusion asks for the camera on this page only. The rest of the site stays camera-off.
/SensorFusion-2525
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), geolocation=(self)

/SensorFusion-2525/
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), geolocation=(self)

/SensorFusion-2525/*
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(self), geolocation=(self)

# The full-screen Sensor Fusion file. Same bytes as the download twin.
/sensor-fusion/SensorFusion-2525.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(self)

# Download twin. Content-Disposition is what makes a phone save the file.
/sensor-fusion/download/SensorFusion-2525.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="SensorFusion-2525.html"

# Same save function as Vision-2525. The phone saves the program instead of showing it.
/sensor-fusion/download/sensor_fusion_edge.py
  Cache-Control: no-cache, must-revalidate
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8
  Content-Disposition: attachment; filename="sensor_fusion_edge.py"

# The pdfjs worker (copied by scripts/copy-pdf-worker.mjs) must load as JavaScript under nosniff —
# a MIME mismatch here is a hard worker-load failure for every PDF render (Athena, wave 1).
# AT THE FOOT since 2026.10.17: it is the one path that wants a long cache and used to sit ABOVE the
# catch-all, where a last-wins resolution would have stripped its day. Below, it wins under either rule.
/pdf.worker.min.mjs
  Content-Type: text/javascript; charset=utf-8
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
